{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":215391,"duration_api_ms":317300,"num_turns":20,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"be30af7f-21ea-4346-88ed-fcaf400b8716","total_cost_usd":1.15172695,"usage":{"input_tokens":6230,"cache_creation_input_tokens":49622,"cache_read_input_tokens":580869,"output_tokens":4165,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":49622,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":6230,"outputTokens":4165,"cacheReadInputTokens":580869,"cacheCreationInputTokens":49622,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.7358470000000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":5718,"outputTokens":19888,"cacheReadInputTokens":1775607,"cacheCreationInputTokens":106529,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.4158799500000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Reviewed the reproduction factors implementation across shrimp.rs, process.rs, biology.rs, neocaridina_davidi.toml, default.toml, and all 22 related tests (16 unit + 6 e2e). All tests pass. The multiplicative 8-factor readiness model (temperature, condition, minerals, stability, molt_stress, density, TAN, nitrite) is well-structured, biologically defensible, and thoroughly tested against all acceptance criteria. Division-by-zero protections are consistently applied. Parameter invariant validation (including strictly-increasing density thresholds) prevents misconfiguration at runtime. Two hardcoded constants in the reproduction code deserve attention, and one inconsistency between molt and reproduction mineral floors could cause surprising behavior if tuned independently.","amendments":[{"body":"In `gh_mineral_factor()` (shrimp.rs:1639-1641), the floor value 0.3 is hardcoded in both the low-GH and high-GH branches, while the parallel `molt_mineral_modifier()` (shrimp.rs:1690) uses the tunable `params.molt_mineral_factor_floor`. If a user tunes `molt_mineral_factor_floor` to a different value, molt and reproduction GH penalties will diverge silently. Either: (a) add a dedicated `repro_mineral_factor_floor` parameter, or (b) reuse `molt_mineral_factor_floor` in `gh_mineral_factor()` and document that it controls both domains.","rationale":"This is an inconsistency between two subsystems that use the same biological concept (minimum GH impact). A user tuning one will expect the other to follow. The hardcoded 0.3 is currently correct by coincidence (it matches the default), but will surprise anyone who adjusts the configurable parameter."},{"body":"In `tan_repro_factor()` (shrimp.rs:1619) and `no2_repro_factor()` (shrimp.rs:1630), the slope of suppression is computed as `threshold * 2.0`, meaning full suppression always occurs at 2× the threshold concentration. This implicit doubling rule is not exposed as a tunable parameter. Add explicit `tan_repro_full_suppression_mg_n_per_l` and `no2_repro_full_suppression_mg_n_per_l` parameters (defaulting to 2.0 and 1.0 respectively) so the suppression curve shape can be tuned independently of the onset threshold.","rationale":"The acceptance criteria require that 'Temperature, density, instability, and chemistry suppression factors are all named parameters in species/process data.' The onset thresholds are named, but the slope/ceiling of the TAN and NO2 curves is controlled by a hardcoded multiplier. The test `test_all_reproduction_factors_are_named_parameters` validates that thresholds change behavior, but does not verify that the suppression curve shape itself is tunable. This is a partial gap against the 'named parameters' criterion."},{"body":"In `temp_repro_factor()` (shrimp.rs:1546-1549), the cold-temperature offset `4.0` (which defines the width of the ramp from 0.1 to 1.0 below `optimal_temp_min_c`) is hardcoded. Similarly, the piecewise slopes 0.5 and 0.45 (lines 1554, 1557) and the minimum values 0.1 and 0.05 (lines 1547, 1559) are all hardcoded. These shape the temperature-reproduction curve but cannot be tuned via species data. At minimum, document these as intentionally fixed; ideally, extract the cold-offset width as a named parameter.","rationale":"Same 'named parameters' criterion. The hot-side parameters (penalty_start, penalty_full) are tunable and tested, but the cold-side curve shape is fixed. This is lower severity since most Neocaridina tanks are warm, but it limits the model's reusability for other species."},{"body":"In `gh_mineral_factor()` (shrimp.rs:1641), the high-GH penalty divisor is hardcoded as `10.0`: `(1.0 - (gh_d - params.gh_max_d) / 10.0)`. The molt system has a tunable `molt_gh_excess_penalty_divisor` for the analogous calculation. Use a corresponding named parameter for the reproduction GH excess curve.","rationale":"Same pattern as the floor inconsistency — molt and reproduction use different mechanisms for the same biological concept (high-GH penalty). The hardcoded 10.0 divisor means the reproduction GH penalty slope is fixed at -0.1 per dGH above max, regardless of any molt-side tuning."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"6b0d4196-c056-434c-b861-a795f7ffb9d6"}
